course: dictionary
lesson: 11422
title: Agreeing without a fight
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words tell you how a person responds to a choice someone else has made. Acquiesce is one of them. To acquiesce means to agree quietly, or to accept something without arguing, even when you are not keen. «Mum wanted fish for tea, so I acquiesced, though I would much rather have had pasta.» The clue is «though I would rather have had pasta»: I did not really want it, but I did not make a fuss.
explain: Acquiesce is a formal word, so you will often meet it in history books, exam texts and grown-up writing. People acquiesce to rules, demands or decisions. In a story, a character might acquiesce with a sigh, which tells you they gave in unwillingly. When you meet it, look for clues like a sigh, a shrug or the word «though», which often show the agreement was reluctant.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. «She acquiesced to the plan» becomes «She agreed quietly to the plan», and if that still makes sense, you have the right meaning. In everyday talk you could say «go along with», «give in» or «agree» instead.
fact: Acquiesce is a verb, and its forms are acquiesces, acquiesced and acquiescing.
fact: «Acquiesce» means to agree quietly, or to accept something without arguing.
fact: You can acquiesce even if you are not keen; the key idea is that you do not argue.
fact: Similar words are «give in», «go along with» and «agree», but acquiesce sounds more formal.
fact: Two common phrases are «acquiesce to» and «acquiesce in», as in «She acquiesced to the plan.»
exercise: «She acquiesced to the plan, though she had wanted a different one.» What does acquiesced mean? | Ran away from the plan | Shouted loudly against the plan | *Agreed quietly, even though she was not keen | Wrote a brand new plan
exercise: «He acquiesced with a sigh when his sister chose the film.» Which word is a clue that he was not keen? | *The sigh | The cinema | The popcorn | The long queue
exercise: Ella says acquiesce means to argue loudly until you win. Is she right? | No, it means to lose your temper | Yes, it means to win every game | *No, it means to agree quietly without arguing | Yes, it means to shout a lot
test: «The council acquiesced to the residents' demand for a new bus stop.» What is the best meaning? | Asked for more money | Built a new road | Refused angrily | *Agreed without a fight
test: «Tom acquiesced, but he grumbled all the way to bed.» What does acquiesced mean here? | *Agreed, even though he was not happy about it | Argued until midnight | Ran upstairs quickly | Stayed up late happily
exam: Which phrase means the same as «She acquiesced to the rule»? | Forgot the rule | Made up a new rule | Broke the rule | *Gave in to the rule
